<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is allowed on purpose, for every host.

  This is an IPTV player: the streams it plays are hosted by third parties it
  has never heard of, and a large share of them are plain http. Measured
  against the twelve default lists, 542 of 4.242 streams (12,8%) are http,
  spread over 118 different hosts — many of them bare IP addresses that rotate.
  There is no domain allowlist that can cover that.

  The previous config listed the hosts the *playlists* come from and blocked
  everything else, which is a different thing entirely: Android refused the
  connection before it was attempted and the player reported
  ERROR_CODE_IO_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED. Those were live channels the app was
  rejecting on its own.

  The tradeoff is real and accepted: cleartext streams can be read or tampered
  with in transit. The alternative is silently dropping one channel in eight.
  No credentials are ever sent over these connections; Xtream accounts are the
  user's own and their URL scheme is whatever the user configured.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
